---
<ArticleLayout
  title="Whole House Generator Cost (2026): Installed Prices"
  description="How much does a whole house generator cost in 2026? Compare model MSRPs, published installed-price ranges, installation costs and quote scope."
  category="Cost" categoryHref="/cost/" updated="2026-10-04" verified="2026-10-04" image="/images/cost-install-quote.webp" readingTime="10 min read" toc={toc}
  takeaways={[
    'A manufacturer-listed generator MSRP is equipment pricing—not a complete installed-system price.',
    'Published installed ranges vary materially by source and scope; a site-specific, itemized local quote is the useful comparison.',
    'Fuel-line, electrical, transfer-switch, site-preparation, permit, and panel requirements can change the project.',
  ]}
  sources={[
    { label: 'Generac: home standby installation process and cost range', href: 'https://www.generac.com/resources/home-management/backup-generator-installation/', type: 'Manufacturer', verified: '2026-10-04' },
    { label: 'HomeGuide: whole-house generator cost (published Dec. 30, 2025)', href: 'https://homeguide.com/costs/generator-cost', type: 'Industry', verified: '2026-10-04' },
    ...pricedModels.flatMap((model) => model.sources.map((source) => ({ label: source.label, href: source.url, type: 'Manufacturer' as const, verified: source.checkedOn }))),
  ]}
  related={[
    { href: '/cost/installation-cost-breakdown/', title: 'Whole house generator installation cost', description: 'The work categories that should appear in a written proposal.', category: 'Cost' },
    { href: '/cost/transfer-switch-cost/', title: 'Transfer-switch cost', description: 'Compare switch scope only after matching rating and equipment.', category: 'Cost' },
    { href: '/installation/what-to-expect/', title: 'What to expect during installation', description: 'Plan the site assessment, permits, work, and commissioning.', category: 'Installation' },
  ]}
  prev={{ href: '/sizing/what-size-generator-do-i-need/', label: 'Sizing method' }}
  next={{ href: '/cost/installation-cost-breakdown/', label: 'Installation scope' }}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick answer: how much does a whole house generator cost?</h2>
    <p><strong>Published whole house generator cost estimates vary because the sources do not measure identical project scope.</strong> Generac currently publishes a broad <strong>$8,000–$16,000</strong> range including its generator, system equipment/materials, and installation. HomeGuide, in a guide published December 30, 2025, lists an average <strong>$6,000–$11,000</strong> total for a whole-house generator and installation, and <strong>$9,500–$24,000</strong> for longer distances or systems above 20 kW.</p>
    <p>Those figures are planning references from different sources—not a Standby Generator Guide national average and not a quote for your property. The actual installed price depends on the exact generator and transfer equipment, fuel work, electrical work, site preparation, permits, inspections, local labor, and any service-panel or utility upgrades.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="price-scope">
    <h2>Whole house generator cost: separate equipment from installation</h2>
    <p><strong>Whole house generator cost depends on much more than the generator itself.</strong> A product page may show a starting MSRP, but a completed project can also involve a transfer switch, wiring/conduit, fuel connection or tank, site/pad preparation, permits, inspections, startup, and related labor. Which items are included depends on the model/package, property, jurisdiction, and installer. A headline unit price cannot establish the full installed cost.</p>
    <p>National planning estimates differ. Generac’s installation guide states a broad <strong>$8,000–$16,000</strong> range that includes its generator, system equipment/materials, and installation. HomeGuide, in a guide published December 30, 2025, lists an average <strong>$6,000–$11,000</strong> total for a whole-house generator and installation, and <strong>$9,500–$24,000</strong> for longer distances or systems above 20 kW. These are sources’ different estimates—not a blended “national average,” a bid, or a promise about any one property.</p>
    <p>Use the ranges to decide what questions to ask; use current local proposals to budget the actual project. Standby Generator Guide does not sell generators or arrange installer quotes.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="current-msrp">
    <h2>Selected current model MSRPs checked October 4, 2026</h2>
    <p>The following are exact manufacturer model-page starting prices, where the page provides one. They are not equivalent system prices: the switch/bundle scope differs or is not established on some pages. Product pricing, availability, and bundle contents can change.</p>
    <div class="not-prose mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[#d9e0ea]">
      <table class="mobile-card-table sm:min-w-[700px] w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <caption class="sr-only">Selected manufacturer-listed starting prices for exact residential standby generator models</caption>
        <thead class="bg-[#edf3fb] text-primary"><tr><th scope="col" class="p-3">Exact model</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">Manufacturer-listed starting MSRP</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">Scope note from source</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-800">
          {pricedModels.map((model) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="p-3 font-semibold"><a href={model.productUrl} rel="external noopener noreferrer" class="text-primary underline decoration-accent underline-offset-4">{model.brand} {model.modelId}</a><span class="mt-1 block text-sm font-normal">{model.displayName}</span></th>
              <td class="p-3">${model.startingMsrpUsd?.toLocaleString('en-US')} USD</td>
              <td class="p-3">{model.startingMsrpNote}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3">The KOHLER manufacturer pages show distinct 26RCA and ATS-inclusive 26RCAL starting MSRPs, and Generac model-family sheets describe a 200 A ATS in bundled models. Those examples demonstrate why a starting price must be matched to the exact package. Do not infer dealer inclusions from a headline price—require exact model numbers and components in writing.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="installed-ranges">
    <h2>Published installed ranges: keep source scope visible</h2>
    <div class="not-prose mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[#d9e0ea]">
      <table class="mobile-card-table sm:min-w-[680px] w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <caption class="sr-only">Different published planning ranges for standby generator equipment and installation</caption>
        <thead class="bg-[#edf3fb] text-primary"><tr><th scope="col" class="p-3">Publisher and date</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">Published amount</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">Scope and caveat</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-800">
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Generac installation guide; accessed Oct. 4, 2026</th><td class="p-3">$8,000–$16,000</td><td class="p-3">Generac says this includes its generator, system equipment/materials, and installation; location and complexity vary. Manufacturer estimate, not independent survey data.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">HomeGuide cost guide; published Dec. 30, 2025</th><td class="p-3">$6,000–$11,000 average; $9,500–$24,000 for longer distances or over 20 kW</td><td class="p-3">Guide’s stated whole-house generator plus labor estimates. It separately notes unit-only, install, fuel-line, switch, pad, and permit values; ranges are not additive quote line items.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3">The gap between sources is a reason to compare what each estimate includes, not to average the values. A long gas run, service-panel work, constrained siting, larger capacity, or unusual permitting can place a project outside a headline planning range.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="what-changes">
    <h2>What changes a quote</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Equipment scope:</strong> exact model, fuel rating, standby output, transfer switch and service rating, load management, monitoring, battery, and any package-specific accessories.</li>
      <li><strong>Electrical path:</strong> panel configuration, circuit selection, switch location, wire/conduit length, trenching, panel changes, and commissioning.</li>
      <li><strong>Fuel path:</strong> existing fuel type/capacity, pressure and flow at generator load, pipe sizing, distance, trenching, meter/regulator changes, and propane storage if used.</li>
      <li><strong>Site work:</strong> approved base/pad, grading, drainage, access, clearances, distance to openings, vegetation, service access, and any special lifting needs.</li>
      <li><strong>Local approval:</strong> permits, inspections, utility coordination, property/HOA restrictions, and local code requirements.</li>
      <li><strong>Labor and service:</strong> contractor availability, included startup/transfer testing, warranty registration, and who provides future service.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="line-items">
    <h2>Published planning references for individual line items</h2>
    <p>HomeGuide lists the following broad planning figures in its cited cost guide. They are not guaranteed local prices, and the items should <strong>not</strong> simply be added together: estimates may overlap with each other or with an installer’s bundled scope.</p>
    <div class="not-prose mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[#d9e0ea]">
      <table class="mobile-card-table sm:min-w-[610px] w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <caption class="sr-only">Selected HomeGuide cost factors with their scope qualifiers</caption>
        <thead class="bg-[#edf3fb] text-primary"><tr><th scope="col" class="p-3">Cost component</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">Published planning reference</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">Scope caveat</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-800">
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Automatic transfer switch installation</th><td class="p-3">$600–$2,500</td><td class="p-3">HomeGuide reference; exact switch, rating, service equipment, wiring, and labor affect cost.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Electrical wiring</th><td class="p-3">$6–$8 per foot</td><td class="p-3">Guide’s range; route, conductors, trench, and local labor may differ.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Gas line installation</th><td class="p-3">$12–$25/ft in the cost-factor table; $15–$50+/ft in the narrative note</td><td class="p-3">HomeGuide gives inconsistent ranges within the same guide; use only as rough context and request local pricing for the specified pipe, route, trenching, and fuel demand.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Precast concrete pad</th><td class="p-3">$150–$400</td><td class="p-3">Guide’s reference; site work and approved base requirements may change the scope.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Propane tank installation</th><td class="p-3">$400–$1,800</td><td class="p-3">Guide’s reference; tank size, ownership, burial, fill, and regulation change the proposal.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Permits</th><td class="p-3">$80–$450</td><td class="p-3">Guide’s average; local permit types and fees vary.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3">Source and update date: <a href="https://homeguide.com/costs/generator-cost" rel="external noopener noreferrer">HomeGuide’s whole-house generator cost guide</a>, published December 30, 2025; accessed October 4, 2026.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="quote-checklist">
    <h2>Compare quotes with this checklist</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Are the generator model number, fuel rating, output, and phase identical?</li>
      <li>Is an ATS/interlock included? Record its model, amperage, and service-entrance rating, if applicable.</li>
      <li>Which circuits and load-management devices are included, and which appliances are excluded or sequenced?</li>
      <li>Are fuel capacity/pressure checks, line work, regulator/meter changes, and leak testing included?</li>
      <li>Are wiring, trenching, panel work, pad/site preparation, permit fees, inspections, and startup/commissioning included?</li>
      <li>Are allowances, exclusions, taxes, payment schedule, warranty responsibilities, and change-order terms stated?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Compare at least two written proposals on the same equipment and scope. Ask each contractor to identify assumptions, exclusions, and any work that depends on a utility or permit authority. For a printable-style side-by-side worksheet, use the dedicated <a href="/cost/standby-generator-quote-checklist/">16-line standby generator quote checklist</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="cost-faq">
    <h2>Common whole house generator cost questions</h2>
    <h3>How much does a whole house generator cost installed?</h3>
    <p>An installed price can include the generator, transfer switch, electrical work, fuel connection, pad or site preparation, permits, inspections, startup, and related labor. Published ranges above provide context, but a site-specific written proposal is the only useful way to price the actual property.</p>

    <h3>What is the average cost of a whole house generator?</h3>
    <p>HomeGuide publishes a $6,000–$11,000 average total in its December 2025 guide, while Generac publishes a different $8,000–$16,000 planning range for its generator, system equipment/materials, and installation. Because the sources use different scope and methodology, we do not blend them into a new average.</p>

    <h3>How much does whole house generator installation add?</h3>
    <p>There is no fixed installation add-on. Fuel-line distance and capacity, electrical routing, transfer equipment, panel work, trenching, pad/site work, permits, and local labor can materially change the project. See the <a href="/cost/installation-cost-breakdown/">whole house generator installation cost breakdown</a> for the separate work categories.</p>

    <h3>Why can two generator quotes be thousands of dollars apart?</h3>
    <p>Two totals may cover different equipment and work. Compare the exact generator and ATS model numbers, backed-up loads, fuel and electrical scope, permits, site work, commissioning, warranty responsibilities, allowances, and exclusions before treating the lower number as the lower-cost project.</p>

    <h3>How much do 22 kW and 26 kW whole house generators cost?</h3>
    <p>Equipment prices depend on the exact model and whether the published price includes transfer equipment or another bundle. The current model-price table above keeps each manufacturer-listed starting MSRP attached to its exact configuration; use that rather than assuming one universal price by kilowatt rating.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="ownership">
    <h2>Fuel and maintenance after installation</h2>
    <p>Fuel use depends on exact model, fuel, load, fuel composition/pressure, and run time. Use the manufacturer’s fuel-consumption table for the actual model and current local utility or propane pricing; a half-load test value is not an hourly household bill. Our <a href="/models/">model data pages</a> show cited test points and conditions.</p>
    <p>Service schedules also differ. Follow the exact generator owner/maintenance manual and service provider’s instructions. HomeGuide lists annual maintenance estimates of $200–$650 in its 2025 guide, but local service plans, included labor, parts, operating hours, and battery replacement can change actual costs. Get a current written service quote rather than treating a national estimate as a required fee. To plan beyond the first year, use our <a href="/cost/standby-generator-ownership-cost/">standby generator ownership-cost worksheet</a> for fuel, maintenance, and 5-/10-year scenarios.</p>
  </section>
